  "result": "The gate returned **OK** on the second of six runs (6 witnesses), and `task_result.md` is written.\n\n**Rule** (`NRS_484B_227_1_o1.lean`):\n- **Who it covers:** `x` must be a vehicle under the definition the node cites (`DEF-NRS-484A-320::vehicle`).\n- **When it applies:** some highway has a median on it, and `x` occupies a roadway or a median on that highway.\n- **The duty:** on any such highway, for every roadway `x` occupies, `x` must not move against that roadway's designated direction. This uses the `against_carrier_direction` concept with `p.speed_resolution` and `p.opposed_direction_cosine`.\n\n**One departure from the record's wording.** The record says the duty re-selects the highway \"with the same selection\", and the first gate run returned VACUOUS on exactly that. The \"x occupies a median\" part of the selection can never change a verdict inside the duty. The duty only speaks about roadways `x` occupies on that highway, and that already implies `x` is on the highway. So the duty now re-selects the highway as \"has a median, and `x` occupies a roadway on it\". The duty covers exactly the same highways as before, so I didn't record a narrowing. The applicability still has both parts. The plan agent could word future records this way to avoid the problem.\n\n**Witnesses:** I built the five planned scenes on a highway with a westbound roadway, an eastbound roadway and a median with an opening. The `blind` scene masks only `st.lane_direction`. I added `branch_2`, a car in the median opening and on no roadway, which expects compliant. It's the only scene where the rule applies because the car is on the median rather than a roadway.\n\nThe node didn't look wrong and no concept misbehaved, so there's no `dependency_defect.json`.",
